🔹 What Happens at Startup (Step-by-Step)

When your application starts:
1️⃣ Spring Boot starts the ApplicationContext
2️⃣ It scans for configuration classes
    It finds your class annotated with @Configuration.
3️⃣ It finds @Bean methods
    It sees:
        @Bean
        public MessageConverter convert()

        @Bean
        public AmqpTemplate getTemplate(ConnectionFactory connection)
4️⃣ Spring calls those methods
Spring executes:
    and stores the returned objects inside the IoC container.
    You don’t see the calls — Spring does it internally.




================================================================================================================================================================


🔹 What Is Actually Happening Internally
    Spring creates a special proxy of annotations
                @Configuration
                @Component
                @Service
                @Repository
                @Controller
                @RestController

    When the container needs a bean:
        It checks if it already exists (singleton by default)
        If not, it calls the method
        Stores the returned object
        Injects it wherever needed

🔹 When Exactly Are They Called?
    They are called:
        During application startup — before your controllers, listeners, etc. start working.
    Specifically:
        Context creation phase
        Bean instantiation phase
        Dependency injection phase


